      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to calculate the product of a sequence of fractions where each fraction's numerator is the same and each fraction's denominator incrementally increases by 1 starting from 4 through to 52. Therefore, the product can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\n\\]\n\nTo find this product, we note that the numerator of each fraction is the same as the denominator of the next fraction. Hence, we can rewrite the product as:\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=1}^{49} \\frac{k}{4+k}\n\\]\n\nAt this point, the terms will have overlying cancels and combine into one fraction. To solve this algebraically, we can simplify the product as step-by-step processes combined in a balanced approach.\n\nFirstly, let’s note the pattern of the cancellation between the numerators and denominators. For each term \\(\\frac{k}{4 + k}\\) in the cycle:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k}{4+k} \\times \\frac{(k+1)}{k+5} = \\frac{k(k+1)}{4k + 4(k+1)} = \\frac{k(k+1)}{4k + 4k + 4} = \\frac{k(k+1)}{8k + 4}\n\\]\n\nFrom here on, the denominator will always be \\(8m + 4\\) where \\(m\\) increases in a cycle. Simplified, the product can be seen to theoretically result to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\left(\\prod_{k=1}^{49} k^{-1}\\right)}{\\left( 4^{-1} \\cdot 7^{-1} \\cdot 10^{-1} \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot 54^{-1} \\right) } = \\frac{1}{\\left( 4 \\times 7 \\times 10 \\times \\ldots \\times 54 \\right)}\n\\]\n\nHence, after multiplying each fraction back-to-back, each simplifying together we get:\n\n\\[\n1 \\div \\frac{4 \\times 7 \\times 10 \\times \\ldots \\times 54}{4} = \\frac{54}{4}\n\\]\n\nNow in finalizing the fraction, we simplify it:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{27}{2}}\n\\]",
